# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROPERTYNEX — Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.
# NEVER commit .env.local — it is already in .gitignore.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PostgreSQL connection strings.
#
# The schema reads BOTH: `url` uses the pooled connection and `directUrl` uses the
# unpooled one, because Prisma needs a direct connection for `db push` and a
# connection pooler cannot serve one.
#
# Vercel Postgres / Neon create both variables for you and they are NOT the same
# string. Running locally there is no pooler, so point both at the same database.
POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/propertynex"
POSTGRES_URL_NON_POOLING="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/propertynex"

# Used to sign/verify session cookies.
# Generate a strong random value, e.g.: openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET="replace-with-a-secure-random-secret"

# Public app URL (used for building absolute links, cookie settings, etc.)
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Separate secret used specifically for password-reset session tokens,
# so a leak of one secret does not compromise the other.
PASSWORD_RESET_SECRET="replace-with-a-secure-reset-secret"

# Keys the HMAC that one-time passcodes are stored under. REQUIRED — there is
# deliberately NO fallback to another secret.
#
# A 6-digit code has only a million possible values, so an unkeyed hash of one is
# trivially reversible; this secret is what makes a database leak insufficient to
# recover a live passcode. It must be a DISTINCT value from AUTH_SECRET and
# PASSWORD_RESET_SECRET: sharing one would mean a single leak compromised session
# cookies, recovery tokens and passcode digests all at once, instead of one of them.
#
# Minimum 16 characters; the app refuses to issue or verify a passcode without it.
OTP_SECRET="replace-with-a-third-secure-random-secret"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OTP DELIVERY PROVIDERS
#
# Password recovery sends a one-time passcode to the email address or mobile
# number on the account. Which service actually delivers it is configured here
# and nowhere else — no credential is hard-coded, and none has a default.
#
# Resolution is fail-closed: if a channel has no provider configured, the
# recovery endpoint returns a generic "temporarily unavailable" error rather
# than pretending the code was sent. See lib/otp/providers/index.ts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Email channel ───────────────────────────────────────────
# OTP_EMAIL_PROVIDER = console | resend
#   console : development only — prints the passcode to the server console.
#             Refuses to run with NODE_ENV=production.
#   resend  : real delivery via the Resend HTTP API (no SDK dependency).
# Unset in development defaults to `console`; unset in production is an error.
OTP_EMAIL_PROVIDER="console"

# Required when OTP_EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# RESEND_API_KEY="re_..."
# OTP_EMAIL_FROM="PROPERTYNEX <no-reply@yourdomain.com>"

# ── SMS channel ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# OTP_SMS_PROVIDER = console | twilio
OTP_SMS_PROVIDER="console"

# Required when OTP_SMS_PROVIDER=twilio
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID="AC00000000000000000000000000000000"
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN="your-twilio-auth-token"
# OTP_SMS_FROM="+15550000000"

# Set to "false" to stop the development console provider from printing
# passcodes (useful for exercising the delivery-failure path locally).
# Has no effect in production, where that provider cannot be selected at all.
# OTP_DEV_LOG_CODES="true"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PROPERTY MEDIA STORAGE
#
# Where uploaded property photos are written. The application never builds a URL
# from any of this: a stored file is only ever addressed as /api/media/<id>, which
# resolves the row, checks who may see it, and streams the bytes. So changing the
# driver changes nothing a browser sees, and no internal path is ever published.
#
# Resolution is fail-closed, the same posture as the OTP providers above: an
# unrecognised driver name is an error at startup, not a silent fall back to the
# local disk. A deployment that believed it was writing to a bucket while actually
# writing to an ephemeral container filesystem would lose every photo on the next
# restart, quietly. See lib/media/storage/index.ts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER = local | vercel-blob
#   local       : the server's own filesystem, under MEDIA_STORAGE_DIR.
#   vercel-blob : Vercel Blob object storage.
#
# LEAVE IT UNSET and the right driver is chosen for where the code is running:
# `vercel-blob` when Vercel sets VERCEL=1, `local` everywhere else. Setting it
# explicitly still wins — except `local` on Vercel, which is refused rather than
# obeyed, because it cannot work there (see below).
#
# `local` is correct for a single-instance deployment and it is NOT correct for a
# horizontally scaled or serverless one: each instance would hold a different
# subset of the photos, and a container's disk does not survive a restart. On
# Vercel it does not work at all — a function's filesystem is read-only outside
# /tmp, so the first upload fails with
# `ENOENT: no such file or directory, mkdir '/var/task/.media-storage'`. No value
# of MEDIA_STORAGE_DIR fixes that, because the fault is the filesystem and not the
# path.
#
# Credentials for a remote driver belong here, as environment variables, and
# nowhere else. Nothing in the codebase reads a key or a bucket name from anywhere
# but process.env.
# MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER="local"

# Root directory for the `local` driver. Relative paths resolve from the project
# root; the directory is created on first upload. Ignored by every other driver.
#
# Deliberately NOT inside public/ — anything there is served by the static handler,
# which runs no access check, so an unpublished listing's photos would be
# world-readable the moment they were uploaded. Defaults to .media-storage, which
# is in .gitignore.
MEDIA_STORAGE_DIR=".media-storage"

# ── Vercel Blob (the `vercel-blob` driver) ──────────────────
#
# Create a Blob store and connect it to the project (Vercel dashboard → Storage →
# your store → Projects → Connect to Project). Connecting sets BLOB_STORE_ID and a
# short-lived, automatically rotated VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN, and the SDK reads both by
# itself — there is nothing to paste and no long-lived secret to leak.
#
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN is the fallback, for code that runs outside Vercel (a CI
# job, another host, or a local machine that has not run `vercel env pull`). It is
# a long-lived credential: put it in .env.local, never in this file, and never in
# a commit. The app checks only that one of the two is PRESENT; it never logs or
# echoes either value.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN="vercel_blob_rw_..."

# Access mode of the connected store. Defaults to `private`, which is the mode
# that keeps the app's privacy contract: a public blob is served straight off the
# CDN by its URL with no application code in the path, so an unpublished listing's
# photos would be readable by anyone holding the URL — the same objection that
# keeps the local driver's root out of public/. Private bytes still reach the
# browser only through /api/media/<id>, which checks who may see them first.
#
# A store's access mode is fixed when the store is created and cannot be changed,
# so this must MATCH the store you connected. Only set it to `public` if that is
# what your store is.
# MEDIA_BLOB_ACCESS="private"

# Upload limits (accepted formats, per-file size, per-listing count) are NOT
# configurable here, for the same reason the passcode policy is not: they are the
# validation boundary, not deployment config. They live in lib/media/constants.ts.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AI PROPERTY ASSISTANT  (OPTIONAL — the app works without it)
#
# Enables the natural-language property search panel on /explore, /buy and
# /rent. When not configured, the panel is silently absent and all manual
# filters keep working exactly as before. There is no fallback to a mock or a
# degraded mode — absent is absent.
#
# Resolution is fail-closed and deliberate: leaving AI_PROVIDER blank or unset
# is "not configured" (panel hidden, no error). Setting AI_PROVIDER to a
# recognised name with a missing credential is a misconfiguration — the /api/health
# endpoint will report which variable is missing so it can be fixed, and the
# panel is hidden in the meantime.
#
# AI calls happen server-side only. AI_API_KEY is NOT prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_
# and this module imports `server-only`, so the key cannot reach a browser
# bundle. Do not add NEXT_PUBLIC_ — it would publish the key to every visitor.
#
# ── Supported providers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# anthropic
#   Anthropic's Messages API. AI_BASE_URL defaults to https://api.anthropic.com
#   (overridable for a gateway). AI_MODEL defaults to claude-opus-5.
#   Only AI_API_KEY is strictly required.
#
# openai-compatible
#   Any service that speaks the OpenAI chat-completions wire format: OpenAI,
#   Azure OpenAI, self-hosted servers, Groq, Ollama, etc. AI_BASE_URL and
#   AI_MODEL are both required — there is no sensible default for either.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which provider to use. Leave blank (or omit) to disable the assistant.
# AI_PROVIDER=

# API key for the configured provider.
# AI_API_KEY=

# Base URL for the provider's API.
# For anthropic: defaults to https://api.anthropic.com (only set this for a
#   gateway or proxy).
# For openai-compatible: REQUIRED — no default. Examples:
#   https://api.openai.com/v1          (OpenAI)
#   https://api.groq.com/openai/v1     (Groq)
#   http://localhost:11434/v1          (Ollama)
# AI_BASE_URL=

# Model to use with the configured provider.
# For anthropic: defaults to claude-opus-5 (any claude-* model works).
# For openai-compatible: REQUIRED — no default. Examples:
#   gpt-4o, gpt-4o-mini               (OpenAI)
#   llama-3.3-70b-versatile           (Groq)
#   llama3.2                           (Ollama)
# AI_MODEL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Maps  (OPTIONAL — the app works without it)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Browser key for the Google Maps JavaScript API (the Explore map view) and the
# Maps Embed API (the map on a listing's page).
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is correct and deliberate here. Both are browser products: the key
# travels in a <script src> / <iframe src> and is visible to anyone who opens
# devtools. That is how Google designs them. A browser key is therefore protected
# by RESTRICTION, not secrecy — in the Google Cloud console:
#
#   1. Application restrictions → HTTP referrers → add your domains only.
#   2. API restrictions → allow only "Maps JavaScript API" and "Maps Embed API".
#
# An unrestricted key can be lifted from your page and billed to your account.
#
# LEAVE IT UNSET and the app degrades on purpose rather than breaking:
#   - Explore's Map view renders a panel saying the list is the complete view, and
#     neither the map chunk nor the Google SDK is downloaded.
#   - A listing page shows its locality and precision note instead of a map frame.
#   - "Get directions" and "View on Google Maps" keep working everywhere, because
#     they are plain google.com/maps URLs that need no key and no SDK.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=""

# ── Nearby places ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There is deliberately NO variable for this. "1.2 km to the metro" is only worth
# showing if it is measured, so the nearby-places section is driven by an
# installed provider rather than a flag — an env flag can be set with nothing
# behind it, which is how a UI ends up promising data it cannot fetch.
#
# No provider ships with the app, so the section is absent. To add one, implement
# `NearbyProvider` and call `installNearbyProvider()` from server startup; put its
# (billed, server-only) API key in a variable WITHOUT the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix so it
# never reaches the browser. See lib/maps/nearby.ts.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Password recovery is OTP-based. There is intentionally no security-question
# configuration here: security answers are low-entropy, often discoverable from
# public information, and prove nothing about present control of the contact
# address an account is registered to. The legacy User.securityQuestionId /
# securityAnswerHash columns survive as optional fields for existing rows only
# and are not read by any flow — see lib/auth/security-questions.ts.
#
# Passcode policy (TTL, cooldown, attempt and resend caps) is deliberately NOT
# environment-configurable — it is security policy, not deployment config, and
# lives in lib/otp/config.ts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
